    "text": "**Case A**\n\n- **A1 \u2013 Cannot tell.** The seven-day search covers only the most recent week. More than 30 days have passed since publication, so that search does not cover the time between publication and the burst. Nothing shows whether any requests came before the burst.\n- **A2 \u2013 Cannot tell.** No record shows what inputs the compromise check used, or their as-of time, when it scored the account at 30 minutes. The email-domain check's record does not answer this.\n- **A3 \u2013 No, not by itself.** A payout destination shared with two other accounts is a link worth following. It does not establish common ownership.\n- **A4 \u2013 No.** An outcome channel is listed only for platform invoices, not for payouts. Nothing shows that a victim report on these payouts would have been captured within 30 days, so the silence tells us nothing.\n- **A5 \u2013 Yes.** The burst came 19 minutes after publication, through a residential proxy, with a new fingerprint. In the same burst someone added a webhook, changed the payout destination and sped up payouts. That combination clearly warrants investigation.\n\n**Case B**\n\n- **B1 \u2013 Yes.** Each account has 100 transactions. Recorded chargebacks give D 0 percent and A, B and C 2 percent each.\n- **B2 \u2013 No.** A combined 5 percent across four accounts is an average and does not cap any single account. Chargebacks total 6, so about 14 alerts sit somewhere unknown, and one account could be well above 5 percent.\n- **B3 \u2013 Cannot tell.** No coverage record is shown for the search for the controlling person. Its completeness and matching method cannot be checked, so finding nothing proves nothing.\n- **B4 \u2013 Cannot tell.** Different analysts on different days does not show independence. Both notes may rest on the same underlying evidence.\n- **B5 \u2013 No.** A blocked payment was never completed, so it could not draw a chargeback. The missing chargeback does not make it non-fraud.\n- **B6 \u2013